WEBVTT

00:00:00.317 --> 00:00:04.202
A safe parsing workflow starts by cleaning boundary whitespace.

00:00:04.202 --> 00:00:06.142
Then identify the input shape.

00:00:06.142 --> 00:00:08.802
Apply the parser that matches that shape.

00:00:08.802 --> 00:00:15.172
If the value does not match an expected format, return NULL or flag it for review instead of guessing.

00:00:15.172 --> 00:00:21.122
Parsing converts representation. It does not prove that the underlying business value is accurate.

00:00:21.122 --> 00:00:25.687
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:25.687 --> 00:00:31.572
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:31.572 --> 00:00:33.852
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:33.852 --> 00:00:39.087
In a real setting such as an operations team aligning timestamps across locations,

00:00:39.087 --> 00:00:44.777
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:44.777 --> 00:00:47.017
before discussing syntax.

00:00:47.017 --> 00:00:53.120
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
